window.__BLOG&&window.__BLOG('c1',"<h1 class=\"btitle\">Open and N-Agent Ad Hoc Teams: Graphs, Embeddings and Partner Pools for Rosters That Change</h1><p class=\"bby\">Qihang Li · October 2026 · written from the reading-map notes of this tree</p><nav class=\"btoc\" aria-label=\"Sections\"><button class=\"lnk\" data-go=\"0\">Setting: a team whose roster keeps changing</button><button class=\"lnk\" data-go=\"1\">Roots: type beliefs and the teammate vector</button><button class=\"lnk\" data-go=\"2\">Graphs for open teams: GPL and its two descendants</button><button class=\"lnk\" data-go=\"3\">Partner pools arrive from Overcooked</button><button class=\"lnk\" data-go=\"4\">Controlling N agents at once: NAHT</button><button class=\"lnk\" data-go=\"5\">Out of the simulator: demonstrations, drones and quadrupeds</button><button class=\"lnk\" data-go=\"6\">How the line is measured, and why the numbers do not line up</button><button class=\"lnk\" data-go=\"7\">Open problems, and a reading order</button></nav><div class=\"bbody\"><h2 id=\"b-setting-a-team-whose-roster-keeps-changing\">Setting: a team whose roster keeps changing</h2>\n<p>Picture a small drone team chasing an intruder through a warehouse. Halfway through the chase one drone has to fly home to recharge, and a drone from another vendor, running software nobody on the team has seen, takes its place. Nobody can stop and retrain. The drones that stay must work out what the newcomer does, and the newcomer must be useful at once. This was never a \"team\" in the sense that most cooperative learning assumes, a fixed group trained together. It is a roster that changes while the game is running.</p>\n<p>This post follows a line of 12 papers on that problem: seven that belong to it, and five guests that arrive from two sibling lines (type-based teammate inference, and population training for humans), because the line borrows ingredients from both. I read them as one story: how researchers went from \"one learner, one fixed team\" to \"several learners, a roster that changes, and finally real robots\".</p>\n<p><strong>Ad hoc teamwork (AHT)</strong> is the problem of building one agent that cooperates well with teammates it has never met, with no joint training. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"0\" title=\"AHT position · Ad Hoc Autonomous Agent Teams: Collaboration without Pre-Coordination\">Stone et al. (2010)</button> posed it as a challenge. Most methods that followed still assume the team is fixed once the game starts. Let's define the vocabulary that the rest of the post needs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Controlled agents</strong> are the ones our algorithm trains. <strong>Uncontrolled agents</strong> are teammates we do not train and whose policy we do not know.</li>\n<li>An <strong>open team</strong> is one where agents may join or leave in the middle of an episode.</li>\n<li>A teammate's <strong>type</strong> is a hidden model of its behaviour (for example \"chases the nearest evader\"). A <strong>team embedding</strong> is a vector, computed from an agent's own history, that summarises what its teammates seem to be doing.</li>\n<li><strong>Zero-shot coordination (ZSC)</strong> means cooperating with strangers at first contact, with no adaptation data.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"b-the-setup-in-one-equation\">The setup, in one equation</h3>\n<p>Here is a simplified version of the setting that the later papers share. A team of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math></span> agents plays a cooperative game with a common reward <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_t</annotation></semantics></math></span>. We control <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math></span> of them with a policy <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>; the other <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M-N</annotation></semantics></math></span> follow policies drawn from a set <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math></span> that we do not know in advance. In the open case the roster at step <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t</annotation></semantics></math></span> can also change. The goal is</p>\n<div class=\"mdisp\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mi>θ</mi></munder><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">[</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></munder><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">]</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\max_{\\theta}\\ \\mathbb{E}\\Big[\\sum_{t}\\gamma^{t} r_t\\Big],</annotation></semantics></math></span></div>\n<p>where the expectation runs over teams sampled by a procedure that picks which agents are controlled, which are uncontrolled and when they come and go, and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma</annotation></semantics></math></span> is the usual discount. With <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=1</annotation></semantics></math></span> and a fixed roster this is classic AHT; with all agents controlled it is cooperative multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL). The paper <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"27\" title=\"NAHT · N-Agent Ad Hoc Teamwork\">N-Agent Ad Hoc Teamwork</button> states exactly this nesting, which is why I use it as the frame.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-why-it-is-hard-and-what-the-standard-answers-mis\">Why it is hard, and what the standard answers miss</h3>\n<p>Three obstacles recur. First, you cannot train with the teammates you will meet, so whatever you learn must generalise to unseen policies. Second, the number of teammates changes, so a network with a fixed input size does not fit. Third, in the later papers you may control several agents at once, while the rest of the team is still foreign.</p>\n<p>The standard tools each cover part of this. Cooperative MARL assumes you control every agent and train them together. The type-based line (guess a set of teammate types, update beliefs from observed play, plan against the likely type) assumes one controlled agent. Population training for ZSC builds a crowd of partners, but in two-player games with a fixed roster. The line in this post is what happens when these are stitched together.</p>\n<p>I find it useful to think of three ingredients:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>A teammate model.</strong> Beliefs over types, or a learned vector, so the agent can adapt to who is present.</li>\n<li><strong>A representation that survives changes in team size.</strong> In this line, a graph over agents, or a team embedding that does not depend on how many teammates there are.</li>\n<li><strong>A diverse set of training partners.</strong> Because you cannot see the test teammates, you train against many imagined ones.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"b-the-line-on-one-page\">The line on one page</h3>\n<p>The lineage, as the tree draws it, runs like this. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"2\" title=\"Belief &amp; truth · Belief and Truth in Hypothesised Behaviours\">Belief & truth</button> → <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"23\" title=\"GPL · Towards Open Ad Hoc Teamwork Using Graph-based Policy Learning\">GPL</button> → <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"24\" title=\"GPL (journal) · A General Learning Framework for Open Ad Hoc Teamwork Using Graph-based Policy Learning\">GPL (journal)</button> and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"28\" title=\"CIAO · Open Ad Hoc Teamwork with Cooperative Game Theory\">CIAO</button>; <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"12\" title=\"LIAM · Agent Modelling under Partial Observability for Deep Reinforcement Learning\">LIAM</button> → GPL (journal) and NAHT; GPL → NAHT → <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"114\" title=\"AT-Drone · AT-Drone: Benchmarking Adaptive Teaming in Multi-Drone Pursuit\">AT-Drone</button>; <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"59\" title=\"FCP · Collaborating with Humans without Human Data\">FCP</button> → <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"61\" title=\"MEP · Maximum Entropy Population-Based Training for Zero-Shot Human-AI Coordination\">MEP</button> → <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"31\" title=\"HOLA · Multi-Robot Open Adaptive Teaming Across Unseen Environments, Partners, and Scales\">HOLA</button>; <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"46\" title=\"COLE · Cooperative Open-ended Learning Framework for Zero-shot Coordination\">COLE</button> → AT-Drone. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"29\" title=\"Open HRC · Open Human-Robot Collaboration using Decentralized Inverse Reinforcement Learning\">Open HRC</button> has no solid edge: it joins by topic (openness), not by method.</p>\n<figure class=\"bfig\"><img src=\"assets/img/c1_method.webp\" alt=\"Evolution of the tree: arrows run from a work to the works that build on it.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Evolution of the tree: arrows run from a work to the works that build on it.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Reading the figure: solid arrows mean \"builds on\" or \"reuses a component\", and the dashed ones are comparisons or related-work citations. The figure is busy (some edges pass behind other nodes), so use the edge list in the text. Two edges deserve a warning. NAHT ← GPL is an <em>idea</em> lineage (teammate vectors, using uncontrolled teammates' data), not reused code; NAHT's own builds-on list is IPPO and LIAM. And the HOLA ← NAHT edge in the tree file looks spurious: NAHT is not in HOLA's reference list, and the match is probably a different paper with a similar title.</p>\n<p>The next table sums up what each paper handles, so that the sections below can be read against it.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Paper</th>\n<th>Controlled agents</th>\n<th>Team can change?</th>\n<th>Sees</th>\n<th>Tested on</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Belief &amp; truth (2016, guest)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Teammates' actions in repeated games</td>\n<td>78 repeated 2x2 games</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LIAM (2021, guest)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>No (K fixed policies)</td>\n<td>Own local view</td>\n<td>Speaker-listener, foraging, predator-prey</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GPL (2021)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Yes, mid-episode</td>\n<td>Full state</td>\n<td>LBF, Wolfpack, FortAttack</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FCP (2021, guest)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>No (two players)</td>\n<td>n/a here</td>\n<td>Overcooked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GPL (journal) (2023)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Yes, mid-episode</td>\n<td>Local window; true state only in training</td>\n<td>Same plus Cooperative Navigation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>COLE, MEP (2023, guests)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>No (two players)</td>\n<td>n/a here</td>\n<td>Overcooked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CIAO (2024)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Full state</td>\n<td>LBF, Wolfpack</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NAHT (2024)</td>\n<td>N, resampled per episode</td>\n<td>Number of controlled agents changes per episode</td>\n<td>Own local history</td>\n<td>Bit game, MPE predator-prey, SMAC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Open HRC (2024)</td>\n<td>Policies of all agents learned from demos</td>\n<td>Agents are called in; exits not tested</td>\n<td>Local views</td>\n<td>Firefighting, table assembly, 6 people with a robot arm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AT-Drone (2025)</td>\n<td>Configurable</td>\n<td>Uncontrolled teammates sampled per episode</td>\n<td>Local, continuous actions</td>\n<td>Drone pursuit, simulation first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HOLA (2026)</td>\n<td>Learners on a team</td>\n<td>Yes, mid-episode</td>\n<td>Local, 2.0 m sensing</td>\n<td>Pursuit with drones and quadrupeds</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"b-roots-type-beliefs-and-the-teammate-vector\">Roots: type beliefs and the teammate vector</h2>\n<h3 id=\"b-belief-truth-what-a-type-belief-is-worth\">Belief &amp; truth: what a type belief is worth</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"2\" title=\"Belief &amp; truth · Belief and Truth in Hypothesised Behaviours\">Belief & truth</button> (Albrecht, Crandall and Ramamoorthy, AIJ 2016) is a guest from the sibling post on inferring teammates. It analyses the <em>type-based method</em>: hypothesise types for other agents, update beliefs from their actions, plan against the likely type. It asks four questions: when are the posteriors correct, do priors matter (and can they be computed automatically), can the task still be done with wrong types, and how can a type be tested. The numbers are modest: on a benchmark of 78 repeated 2x2 games (21 no-conflict, 57 conflict) with 10 prior methods and 30 plays per game, priors significantly changed long-run payoff in the scenarios tested; a statistical type test was near 100% accurate on random behaviours (the paper's wording from a figure, with no table) and mixed on adaptive ones.</p>\n<p>What matters for this post is only the <em>game model</em>. The stochastic Bayesian game defined here is what GPL extends with openness. GPL does not reuse the paper's analysis of beliefs, priors or the type test.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-liam-a-teammate-vector-from-your-own-view\">LIAM: a teammate vector from your own view</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"12\" title=\"LIAM · Agent Modelling under Partial Observability for Deep Reinforcement Learning\">LIAM</button> (Papoudakis, Christianos and Albrecht, NeurIPS 2021) is the second guest, and the source of the idea that the policy can be conditioned on a learned teammate vector. One controlled agent faces others who each use one of K fixed policies, whose identity is never revealed. An encoder maps the agent's own observations and actions to a vector <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math></span>. During training a decoder must rebuild the modelled agent's observation and action from <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math></span>; at execution only the encoder is kept, and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math></span> feeds an A2C policy.</p>\n<p>The paper tests on speaker-listener, level-based foraging (LBF) and predator-prey, with 10 fixed policies each and 5 seeds. It has no table of final numbers; reading the curves, LIAM sits closer to the full-information upper bound (FIAM, which sees the other agent) than to the no-model baseline in all three games, and the paper states over 90% accuracy for the other agent's action after 10 steps in double speaker-listener. Close pairs in the curves are approximate readings.</p>\n<h2 id=\"b-graphs-for-open-teams-gpl-and-its-two-descendant\">Graphs for open teams: GPL and its two descendants</h2>\n<h3 id=\"b-gpl-the-first-learner-designed-for-open-teams\">GPL: the first learner designed for open teams</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"23\" title=\"GPL · Towards Open Ad Hoc Teamwork Using Graph-based Policy Learning\">GPL</button> (Rahman, Hopner, Christianos and Albrecht, ICML 2021) is the first algorithm the authors claim to have designed for <strong>open ad hoc teamwork</strong>. It formalises the problem by extending the stochastic Bayesian game of <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"2\" title=\"Belief &amp; truth · Belief and Truth in Hypothesised Behaviours\">Belief & truth</button> to allow openness (the open stochastic Bayesian game, OSBG), and combines coordination graphs with relational forward models.</p>\n<p>The mechanism has three pieces. Two LSTMs map each agent's history to a type vector. A fully connected coordination graph gives the joint action value as individual terms plus pairwise terms, so the number of terms grows quadratically with team size, not as <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^k</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span> interacting agents. A relational forward model predicts each teammate's action probabilities, assuming teammates act independently, and the value is averaged over those predictions. In schematic form the value looks like</p>\n<div class=\"mdisp\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mo>≈</mo><mtext>  </mtext><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mo>+</mo><mtext>  </mtext><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(\\mathbf{a}) \\;\\approx\\; \\sum_i Q_i(a_i) \\;+\\; \\sum_{i&lt;j} Q_{ij}(a_i,a_j),</annotation></semantics></math></span></div>\n<p>where <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_i</annotation></semantics></math></span> is agent <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i</annotation></semantics></math></span>'s action, <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_i</annotation></semantics></math></span> the individual term and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></span> the pairwise term (the paper uses a low-rank factorisation of the pairwise part; this is my simplified rendering). Two trainers are provided: GPL-Q (Q-learning) and GPL-SPI (soft policy iteration).</p>\n<p>The evidence is Table 2, with teams of up to 5 agents at test time but at most 3 in training (8 seeds, 95% bounds). On FortAttack, GPL-SPI scores 16.82 against 8.12 for the best baseline GNN-AM; on Wolfpack 37.61 against 30.87; on LBF 2.40 against 2.07. The FortAttack margin is large; the other two are smaller and I did not compare their intervals. Two cautions: the baselines MADDPG and DGN were given control of all teammates in training, which gives them more than GPL had; and the main assumption is a <em>fully observed</em> state, with partial views left as future work. A side analysis reports a Pearson correlation of 0.85 between GPL-Q's return and a pairwise utility for shooting in FortAttack. I did not read the appendix proof of the averaging equation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-gpl-journal-partial-views\">GPL (journal): partial views</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"24\" title=\"GPL (journal) · A General Learning Framework for Open Ad Hoc Teamwork Using Graph-based Policy Learning\">GPL (journal)</button> (Rahman et al., JMLR 2023) is the same framework extended to <strong>partial observability</strong>. It adds a learned belief on top of GPL's graph networks that infers hidden state, unseen teammates and their actions. Three variants exist: AE-GPL (recurrent autoencoder), VAE-GPL, and PF-GPL (particle filter with 1, 5 or 10 particles). Belief models are trained to reconstruct true state information that is assumed known during training, which follows the privileged-state training of <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"12\" title=\"LIAM · Agent Modelling under Partial Observability for Deep Reinforcement Learning\">LIAM</button> (the in-tree edge is a \"reuses a component\" one). The full-view results repeat the ICML numbers.</p>\n<p>The partial-view test with unseen teammates (Table 6, 8 seeds) gives:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>LBF</th>\n<th>Wolfpack</th>\n<th>Cooperative Navigation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>VAE-GPL</td>\n<td>0.80</td>\n<td>23.32</td>\n<td>0.17</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AE-GPL</td>\n<td>0.76</td>\n<td>23.78</td>\n<td>0.24</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GPL-Q</td>\n<td>0.77</td>\n<td>21.28</td>\n<td>0.07</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PF-GPL-10</td>\n<td>0.61</td>\n<td>18.12</td>\n<td>0.02</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DVRL</td>\n<td>0.07</td>\n<td>19.29</td>\n<td>-0.11</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PPO</td>\n<td>0.03</td>\n<td>18.43</td>\n<td>-0.24</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The headline \"0.80 against 0.03 for PPO\" is real but needs reading: on Wolfpack the drop of PPO and DVRL is small, so the collapse is specific to LBF and Cooperative Navigation. With seen teammates and different team sizes (Table 5) the paper itself notes no clear winner, DVRL has the highest LBF mean with a wide interval, PF-GPL is the weakest, and confidence intervals overlap in places. The stated limits: teammates have fixed policies, actions are discrete, and training takes millions of steps. In the later literature this paper is cited as related work: NAHT groups it with GPL among open-system methods that adapt only a single agent, and Open HRC calls its setting a toy-domain approach with no real-robot test.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-ciao-why-a-pairwise-value-works\">CIAO: why a pairwise value works</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"28\" title=\"CIAO · Open Ad Hoc Teamwork with Cooperative Game Theory\">CIAO</button> (Wang, Li, Zhang, Pan and Kaski, ICML 2024) starts from a complaint: GPL's joint Q-value over a coordination graph \"lacks convincing explanations\". It supplies one from cooperative game theory. It defines an open stochastic Bayesian coalitional affinity game (OSB-CAG) with a solution concept called the dynamic variational strict core (DVSC, a stable temporary team), and proves in its Theorem 2 that the optimal joint Q-value is a sum of agents' preferences, that is pairwise plus individual utilities. That matches GPL's form, with one new requirement: the pairwise utility must be non-negative. The practical change is a regularizer added to GPL's DQN loss, with a symmetry constraint on a star graph (CIAO-S) or a complete graph (CIAO-C).</p>\n<p>The experiments use LBF and Wolfpack, training with at most 3 agents and testing with at most 5 and 9 (5 seeds, 95% CI, baseline GPL-Q). The findings are learning curves, with no table, so I quote no number. CIAO-C beats GPL in most cases; the star graph is better with fewer agents and the complete graph with more; ablations that allow negative pairwise terms are clearly worse; with new agent types the star graph generalises better. The assumptions are strong: fully observable scenarios, non-negative rewards, a symmetric agent graph, and fixed type policies (which the paper says can be relaxed). The authors say the reason for the star-versus-complete difference deserves future study. HOLA and AT-Drone cite it only as related work.</p>\n<h2 id=\"b-partner-pools-arrive-from-overcooked\">Partner pools arrive from Overcooked</h2>\n<p>The sibling post on population training for humans tells the full story of FCP, MEP and COLE in the Overcooked kitchen (the two-player testbed of <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"101\" title=\"Overcooked · On the Utility of Learning about Humans for Human-AI Coordination\">Overcooked</button>). Here I only take what this line borrows from it: a recipe for <em>training against many different partners</em>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-fcp-the-template\">FCP: the template</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"59\" title=\"FCP · Collaborating with Humans without Human Data\">FCP</button> (Strouse et al., NeurIPS 2021) answers \"can we get human-compatible behaviour without human data?\". Stage one trains 32 self-play agents that differ only in random seed, each saved at three checkpoints (initialisation, 50% of final reward, end). Stage two trains the new agent with RL as a best response to this frozen pool (that is 96 partners, the notes' arithmetic). In Table 1 (agent-agent, mean deliveries over 5 seeds and 5 layouts), against a human proxy FCP scores 10.6 (±0.5) against 4.7 (±0.4) for the same method without the early checkpoints. In a study with 114 Prolific participants, FCP teams delivered the most soups (p &lt; 0.001 against the baselines) and were preferred. The setting is a two-player common-payoff game, so there is no openness here. In this tree, FCP matters as the origin of \"diverse training teammates\". NAHT adopts the idea lightly (its teammates come from teams trained by different MARL algorithms and seeds, which the paper says aligns with FCP's findings), MEP follows its scheme, and HOLA uses FCP as a baseline.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-mep-push-the-pool-apart\">MEP: push the pool apart</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"61\" title=\"MEP · Maximum Entropy Population-Based Training for Zero-Shot Human-AI Coordination\">MEP</button> (Zhao et al., AAAI 2023) keeps FCP's two-stage population-then-best-response scheme and its multiple-checkpoint trick. It adds a <strong>population entropy</strong> bonus (the entropy of the mean policy, a lower bound on pairwise diversity, with O(n) cost) to each agent's reward, so the population spreads out; then one agent is trained against it with prioritised sampling that favours partners it does poorly with. In Table 1 the entropy rises with the bonus weight: for Counter Circuit, 0.988 at weight 0, 1.151 at 0.01, 1.791 at 0.04. The reward results are only bar charts: top in all 5 layouts with a human proxy, and averaged over layouts ahead of SP, PBT, FCP and TrajeDi with real humans on Amazon Mechanical Turk, on par with human-human play. I found no participant count, and no numeric reward is quoted. In this tree its contribution is HOLA's pre-training objective.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-cole-train-on-the-partners-no-one-pairs-with\">COLE: train on the partners no one pairs with</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"46\" title=\"COLE · Cooperative Open-ended Learning Framework for Zero-shot Coordination\">COLE</button> (Li et al., ICML 2023) is the weakest guest here; it joins through a single reuse edge, from AT-Drone. It treats a two-player game as a graph of strategies (edge weight is the expected result of two strategies playing together), keeps each node's preferred partner, and defines <strong>preference centrality</strong>: 0 if every other strategy prefers it, 1 if none does. Each generation trains a best response to the strategies with high centrality, that is, those no one pairs with, and adds it to the population. With expert partners (Table 1, 1:3 partner-sampling ratio) it is best in four of five layouts, for example 212.80 on Cramped Room (best baseline PBT 209.83) and 105.84 on Counter Circuit (best baseline MEP 76.67), and slightly behind MEP on Asymmetric Advantages (178.80 against 179.83). Human-proxy results are bars I could not read, and my reading is that no real humans were tested. COLE reimplements FCP and runs MEP's code only as baselines. AT-Drone later extends its centrality to a hypergraph.</p>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlling-n-agents-at-once-naht\">Controlling N agents at once: NAHT</h2>\n<p>Everything so far controls one agent. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"27\" title=\"NAHT · N-Agent Ad Hoc Teamwork\">NAHT</button> (Wang et al., NeurIPS 2024) names the next step. Real teams mix agents from different makers, so a subteam of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math></span> agents we train must cooperate with unknown uncontrolled teammates, and <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math></span> itself changes from episode to episode. The paper's motivating example is a 3-agent bit game in which a policy that is optimal for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=1</annotation></semantics></math></span> is suboptimal for <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=2</annotation></semantics></math></span> (a win probability of 4/9 against 2/3 for a simple joint policy).</p>\n<p>Its algorithm, <strong>POAM</strong>, is <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"12\" title=\"LIAM · Agent Modelling under Partial Observability for Deep Reinforcement Learning\">LIAM</button> generalised: a recurrent encoder turns an agent's own observation-action history into a team embedding; decoders predict the other agents' observations and actions from it; both policy and critic take the embedding as input. The base is independent PPO (IPPO) with full parameter sharing, chosen because independence avoids dealing with a changing number of agents. Uncontrolled agents' data trains <em>only the critic</em>, as exploration data (an ablation shows that removing it hurts). The paper states that POAM with one controlled agent equals LIAM, which is the cleanest edge in the tree. The diverse training teammates borrow the spirit of <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"59\" title=\"FCP · Collaborating with Humans without Human Data\">FCP</button>: teams trained by VDN, QMIX, IQL, IPPO and MAPPO under different seeds.</p>\n<p>The evidence has two parts. The bit game, Table 3 in the appendix: with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, POAM scores 48.858 (±1.092) and POAM-AHT (the same method trained with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>) 22.5 (±8.250), against an optimum of 50.0; with <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=1</annotation></semantics></math></span> both are about 33.4 (optimum 33.333). On the main tasks (MPE predator-prey, and SMAC 5v6, 8v9, 10v11, 3s5z), five trials and 20M steps, POAM has the best asymptotic return on three of five (predator-prey, 5v6, 3s5z), while IPPO-NAHT ends about equal on 8v9 and 10v11. With unseen teammates (unseen seeds and algorithms) POAM beats IPPO-NAHT on 3 of 5 tasks. These are learning curves, so I quote no per-task returns; the baselines were built by the authors because no earlier method targets this problem. Assumptions to keep in mind: homogeneous controlled agents sharing one policy, no communication, and cooperative teammates.</p>\n<h2 id=\"b-out-of-the-simulator-demonstrations-drones-and-q\">Out of the simulator: demonstrations, drones and quadrupeds</h2>\n<p>The last three papers move toward real systems, and each does so in a different way.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-open-hrc-learn-the-reward-from-demonstrations\">Open HRC: learn the reward from demonstrations</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"29\" title=\"Open HRC · Open Human-Robot Collaboration using Decentralized Inverse Reinforcement Learning\">Open HRC</button> (Suresh et al., IROS 2024) joins the tree by topic, not method. Its parent, Dec-AIRL (decentralised adversarial inverse RL), lies outside the tree. It adds a team ID that can change to a decentralised MDP (the oDec-MDP), and trains oDec-AIRL: a discriminator on (team ID, state, action) separates expert from learner, the shared reward is read off it, and policies are trained with an open-team variant of PPO. Agents join by a CallAgent action; exits are not tested, and teams have at most 3 agents.</p>\n<p>On the benchmarks (averages over 10,000 time steps), Table I gives episode reward 32.22 against 30.37 for closed Dec-AIRL with 2 agents, and 37.86 against 35.73 with 3 agents. In the table-assembly simulation (Table II), the human is active for 13.20 (±0.98) steps against 16.34 (±0.97), and the reward is 4.06 (±0.58) against 1.74 (±0.60). A pilot with 6 people and a Franka arm had all trials succeed, and the text reports an average human time saving of about 18.39%. The paper's Fig. 5 appears to pair the time values differently, which would give smaller savings (about 10% and 17%), so treat 18.39% with care. The baseline is only the closed-system version of the same method, expert data are simulated from human preferences, and it is unclear whether the learner knows the transition functions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-at-drone-a-benchmark-and-naht-in-the-air\">AT-Drone: a benchmark, and NAHT in the air</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"114\" title=\"AT-Drone · AT-Drone: Benchmarking Adaptive Teaming in Multi-Drone Pursuit\">AT-Drone</button> (Li et al., CoRL 2025) is the first dedicated benchmark for adaptive teaming in multi-drone pursuit, according to its authors. It bundles a configurable simulator with four maps (from the easy 4p2e3o to the superhard 4p3e5o: pursuers, evaders and obstacles), a real Crazyflie pipeline, a zoo of seven algorithms, and three zoos of unseen teammates scored by four metrics (success, collisions, average success time, reward). Its tie to the line is two components. <strong>NAHT-D</strong> adapts <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"27\" title=\"NAHT · N-Agent Ad Hoc Teamwork\">NAHT</button> from discrete SMAC actions to continuous steering: MAPPO plus a teammate-modelling autoencoder with embedding size 16. <strong>HOLA-Drone V2</strong>, a baseline from the same group (the earlier single-learner HOLA-Drone is not a member of this tree), extends the preference centrality of <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"46\" title=\"COLE · Cooperative Open-ended Learning Framework for Zero-shot Coordination\">COLE</button> to a preference hypergraph. It also builds on <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"32\" title=\"Other-Play · “Other-Play” for Zero-Shot Coordination\">Other-Play</button> and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"5\" title=\"PLASTIC · Making friends on the fly: Cooperating with new teammates\">PLASTIC</button>, and lists GPL and CIAO as related work limited to discrete actions in simulation.</p>\n<p>The headline: on the hardest map (4p3e5o, Zoo 3), HOLA-Drone V2 has 38.4 collisions against 55.6 for self-play (lower is better; about 31% fewer, my arithmetic), with reward 174.59 against 146.74. But it is not uniform. On Zoo 1 of the same map, V2 has 67.57 collisions and reward 116.57, worse than self-play (62.80, 141.98). NAHT-D without its decoder beats NAHT-D on the two five-obstacle maps (bar charts only), which hints that the decoder may hurt at high complexity. Other caveats: the unit of the collision metric is not stated; Zoo 3 is defined as a mix in the main text but as PPO policies in an appendix; the real arena allows at most 2 evaders while the superhard map has 3; and the real-drone case is qualitative.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-hola-three-kinds-of-change-at-once\">HOLA: three kinds of change at once</h3>\n<p><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"31\" title=\"HOLA · Multi-Robot Open Adaptive Teaming Across Unseen Environments, Partners, and Scales\">HOLA</button> (Li et al., arXiv 2026) asks for adaptation to unseen environments, unseen partners and varying team sizes <em>together</em>, on cooperative pursuit with Crazyflie drones and L1 quadrupeds, deployed without fine-tuning. It models teams as an <strong>Open Hypergraphic-Form Game</strong>: vertices are agent policies, a hyperedge of <span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l</annotation></semantics></math></span> agents is a team, its weight the expected return. Pre-training uses the max-entropy population objective of <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"61\" title=\"MEP · Maximum Entropy Population-Based Training for Zero-Shot Human-AI Coordination\">MEP</button>. In each generation, a Grapher adds learners and scores teams over random layouts, and an Oracle samples partners by the inverse generalised Myerson value (the paper shows this equals a sum of Shapley values), so low-cooperative-capacity partners are sampled more; the learner is then trained as a best response. \"Weak\" here means cooperative capacity, not necessarily low skill.</p>\n<p>The fixed-team study reports 82% success with unseen teammates against 72% for FCP (second best), 13.67% collisions, and episodes of 663.72 steps against 801.19 for FCP; MAPPO and DACOOP-A reach at most 40%. These come from the text of Section VI-A1, since the bars of the other baselines are only in a figure. The margin shrinks on unseen layouts with a fixed team (Table II): success is 44.00 for HOLA against 42.67 for PBT, 40.00 for SP and FCP, 36.00 for MEP. In the open-team study (baselines PBT, SP, MAPPO) the text gives about 73% capture in seen and about 85% in unseen layouts for drones, and 57.2% and 62.6% for quadrupeds; the drone figures are approximate, and it is odd that unseen is higher than seen, a point I cannot explain from the text. The hardware deployments in Fig. 4 have no quantitative results, and the hardware uses motion capture and one central planner. HOLA names MEP for its pool and compares against FCP, and cites the GPL journal paper and CIAO as related work only.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-the-progression-in-one-line\">The progression, in one line</h3>\n<p>Belief &amp; truth, LIAM → GPL (graph value over an open team) → partial views (GPL journal) and game-theoretic constraints (CIAO); LIAM → NAHT (embedding for N agents); FCP → MEP → HOLA (population objective for pre-training); NAHT, COLE → AT-Drone (benchmark with both components). Each arrow answers a limit of the previous node, and each costs an assumption: fixed teammate policies, a known family of teammates, or in the robot papers a motion-capture arena.</p>\n<figure class=\"bfig\"><img src=\"assets/img/c1_tldr.webp\" alt=\"Overview figure of the tree.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Overview figure of the tree.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The small figure keeps the six papers that carry the thread (LIAM, GPL, FCP, CIAO, NAHT, AT-Drone) and bolds GPL and NAHT as the two papers that define the problem and its N-agent generalisation. I would add one caveat on the stage grouping I use: the branches differ in paradigm (value learning, embeddings, population training, imitation), so the grouping is by topic as well as by inheritance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"b-how-the-line-is-measured-and-why-the-numbers-do-\">How the line is measured, and why the numbers do not line up</h2>\n<p>There is no shared benchmark in this line. The papers <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"23\" title=\"GPL · Towards Open Ad Hoc Teamwork Using Graph-based Policy Learning\">GPL</button> and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"28\" title=\"CIAO · Open Ad Hoc Teamwork with Cooperative Game Theory\">CIAO</button> use LBF, Wolfpack and FortAttack; <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"59\" title=\"FCP · Collaborating with Humans without Human Data\">FCP</button>, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"61\" title=\"MEP · Maximum Entropy Population-Based Training for Zero-Shot Human-AI Coordination\">MEP</button> and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"46\" title=\"COLE · Cooperative Open-ended Learning Framework for Zero-shot Coordination\">COLE</button> use Overcooked with human proxies, experts or humans; <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"27\" title=\"NAHT · N-Agent Ad Hoc Teamwork\">NAHT</button> uses a bit game, MPE predator-prey and SMAC; <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"29\" title=\"Open HRC · Open Human-Robot Collaboration using Decentralized Inverse Reinforcement Learning\">Open HRC</button> uses firefighting and assembly; <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"114\" title=\"AT-Drone · AT-Drone: Benchmarking Adaptive Teaming in Multi-Drone Pursuit\">AT-Drone</button> and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"31\" title=\"HOLA · Multi-Robot Open Adaptive Teaming Across Unseen Environments, Partners, and Scales\">HOLA</button> use drone pursuit. Baselines are each paper's own. Here is what each headline number actually measures:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Paper</th>\n<th>What the headline number measures</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Belief &amp; truth</td>\n<td>Long-run payoff change under different priors, 78 repeated 2x2 games (no single score)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LIAM</td>\n<td>Return curves relative to a full-information bound (no table)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GPL</td>\n<td>Return on FortAttack, teams of up to 5, 16.82 vs 8.12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GPL (journal)</td>\n<td>Return on LBF with partial view and unseen teammates, 0.80 vs 0.03 for PPO</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FCP</td>\n<td>Deliveries per episode with a human proxy, 10.6 vs 4.7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MEP</td>\n<td>Reward, top in 5 layouts (bar charts only)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>COLE</td>\n<td>Reward with expert partners, best in 4 of 5 layouts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NAHT</td>\n<td>Return in a bit game with N = 2, 48.9 vs 22.5 (optimum 50.0)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CIAO</td>\n<td>Learning curves vs GPL (no number)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Open HRC</td>\n<td>Steps with the human active in an assembly simulation, 13.2 vs 16.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AT-Drone</td>\n<td>Collisions on one map and one zoo, 38.4 vs 55.6 (lower is better)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HOLA</td>\n<td>Success rate of a fixed team with unseen partners, 82% vs 72% for FCP</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The numbers are therefore meaningful only within a paper, against that paper's own baseline. The most tempting mistake is to compare \"48.9\" with \"82%\". Some further patterns I noticed. Four papers (LIAM, MEP, NAHT's main tasks, CIAO) report results mostly as curves or bar charts, so I have little to quote. Human evidence is thin: FCP (114 people), a pilot of 6 people with a robot arm, and MEP's participant count I could not find; COLE's conference version tests no humans in my reading. GPL, its journal version and CIAO are tested only in simulated games, and the robot papers' headline numbers also come from simulation (HOLA's hardware deployments are shown, not measured).</p>\n<h2 id=\"b-open-problems-and-a-reading-order\">Open problems, and a reading order</h2>\n<h3 id=\"b-what-is-still-open\">What is still open</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Teammates that never learn.</strong> Every method assumes teammates keep one policy while present: <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"12\" title=\"LIAM · Agent Modelling under Partial Observability for Deep Reinforcement Learning\">LIAM</button>, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"23\" title=\"GPL · Towards Open Ad Hoc Teamwork Using Graph-based Policy Learning\">GPL</button>, <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"28\" title=\"CIAO · Open Ad Hoc Teamwork with Cooperative Game Theory\">CIAO</button>, the AT-Drone zoos and HOLA's pool. The GPL journal lists learning teammates as future work, and none of the papers relaxes this.</li>\n<li><strong>Openness is partial.</strong> GPL, CIAO and HOLA let members change mid-episode; NAHT and AT-Drone only resample the number per episode; Open HRC only calls agents in. HOLA assumes a known distribution over team sizes.</li>\n<li><strong>Heterogeneous or communicating controlled agents.</strong> NAHT assumes homogeneous agents with a shared policy and no communication; this is untested rather than shown to fail.</li>\n<li><strong>Real systems stay small.</strong> HOLA uses motion capture and a central planner, AT-Drone's real arena allows at most 2 evaders, and neither reports quantitative real-world results.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence is thin where it matters.</strong> Curves and bar charts without printed numbers (LIAM, MEP, NAHT main tasks, CIAO), baselines built by the authors (NAHT), a baseline that is only the closed-system version of the method itself (Open HRC), and results that are not uniform (AT-Drone's Zoo 1, the GPL journal's seen-teammates Table 5).</li>\n<li><strong>Strong modelling assumptions.</strong> Full state (GPL, CIAO), non-negative rewards and a symmetric graph (CIAO), privileged state in training (GPL journal), a shared reward known to the learner (everything except Open HRC, which learns it from demonstrations).</li>\n<li><strong>The two halves rarely meet in experiments.</strong> HOLA runs FCP and MEP as baselines, but it and AT-Drone cite GPL (journal) and CIAO as related work only, and their baseline lists do not include them. Running the graph-based open-team learners and the partner-pool learners on one benchmark looks like a cheap, informative study.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"b-if-you-only-have-an-afternoon\">If you only have an afternoon</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"23\" title=\"GPL · Towards Open Ad Hoc Teamwork Using Graph-based Policy Learning\">GPL</button> for the problem statement and the first open-team learner; skim <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"24\" title=\"GPL (journal) · A General Learning Framework for Open Ad Hoc Teamwork Using Graph-based Policy Learning\">GPL (journal)</button> only for its partial-view tables.</li>\n<li><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"12\" title=\"LIAM · Agent Modelling under Partial Observability for Deep Reinforcement Learning\">LIAM</button> for the teammate-vector idea that NAHT builds on.</li>\n<li><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"27\" title=\"NAHT · N-Agent Ad Hoc Teamwork\">NAHT</button> for the N-agent formulation; read the bit game first, it explains the point in a page.</li>\n<li><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"59\" title=\"FCP · Collaborating with Humans without Human Data\">FCP</button> (a guest) for the diverse-partner recipe the rest of the line borrows.</li>\n<li><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"31\" title=\"HOLA · Multi-Robot Open Adaptive Teaming Across Unseen Environments, Partners, and Scales\">HOLA</button> for the most complete open-team system so far, then <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"114\" title=\"AT-Drone · AT-Drone: Benchmarking Adaptive Teaming in Multi-Drone Pursuit\">AT-Drone</button> for the benchmark and for how many caveats a real-robot comparison carries.</li>\n<li><button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"28\" title=\"CIAO · Open Ad Hoc Teamwork with Cooperative Game Theory\">CIAO</button> if you want the game-theoretic explanation of why GPL's pairwise value form is natural; <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"29\" title=\"Open HRC · Open Human-Robot Collaboration using Decentralized Inverse Reinforcement Learning\">Open HRC</button> if your interest is learning from human demonstrations.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For the survey vocabulary used above (the open-teams variation, learned agent modelling), see <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"4\" title=\"AHT survey · A Survey of Ad Hoc Teamwork Research\">Mirsky et al. (2022)</button> and <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"3\" title=\"Modelling survey · Autonomous Agents Modelling Other Agents: A Comprehensive Survey and Open Problems\">Albrecht and Stone (2018)</button>. The sibling lines on inferring teammates and on population training for humans are covered in their own posts.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-how-these-numbers-were-handled\">How these numbers were handled</h3>\n<p>Every figure above comes from the paper it is attributed to. I flagged the ones that are approximate (HOLA's open-team percentages, LIAM's curve ordering), derived (my arithmetic: 96 partners, about 31% fewer collisions), or inconsistent inside the paper (Open HRC's 18.39% against its Fig. 5; AT-Drone's Zoo 3 definition). Venue and year follow the file name of the PDF I read; for MEP the PDF is the arXiv v3 preprint (venue from file metadata), for Open HRC it is the MERL report version, and HOLA is an arXiv preprint. Proofs were not checked, I did not check any code repositories, and human-study sizes are what the papers report.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-references\">References</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Stone et al., 2010. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Ad%20Hoc%20Autonomous%20Agent%20Teams%3A%20Collaboration%20without%20Pre-Coordination\">Ad Hoc Autonomous Agent Teams: Collaboration without Pre-Coordination</a>. <em>AAAI 2010</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"0\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Albrecht et al., 2016. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Belief%20and%20Truth%20in%20Hypothesised%20Behaviours\">Belief and Truth in Hypothesised Behaviours</a>. <em>AIJ 2016</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"2\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Barrett et al., 2017. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Making%20friends%20on%20the%20fly%3A%20Cooperating%20with%20new%20teammates\">Making friends on the fly: Cooperating with new teammates</a>. <em>AIJ 2017</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"5\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Albrecht &amp; Stone, 2018. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Autonomous%20Agents%20Modelling%20Other%20Agents%3A%20A%20Comprehensive%20Survey%20and%20Open%20Problems\">Autonomous Agents Modelling Other Agents: A Comprehensive Survey and Open Problems</a>. <em>AIJ 2018</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"3\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Carroll et al., 2019. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=On%20the%20Utility%20of%20Learning%20about%20Humans%20for%20Human-AI%20Coordination\">On the Utility of Learning about Humans for Human-AI Coordination</a>. <em>NeurIPS 2019</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"101\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Hu et al., 2020. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=%E2%80%9COther-Play%E2%80%9D%20for%20Zero-Shot%20Coordination\">“Other-Play” for Zero-Shot Coordination</a>. <em>ICML 2020</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"32\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Papoudakis et al., 2021. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Agent%20Modelling%20under%20Partial%20Observability%20for%20Deep%20Reinforcement%20Learning\">Agent Modelling under Partial Observability for Deep Reinforcement Learning</a>. <em>NeurIPS 2021</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"12\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Rahman et al., 2021. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Towards%20Open%20Ad%20Hoc%20Teamwork%20Using%20Graph-based%20Policy%20Learning\">Towards Open Ad Hoc Teamwork Using Graph-based Policy Learning</a>. <em>ICML 2021</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"23\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Strouse et al., 2021. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Collaborating%20with%20Humans%20without%20Human%20Data\">Collaborating with Humans without Human Data</a>. <em>NeurIPS 2021</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"59\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Mirsky et al., 2022. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A%20Survey%20of%20Ad%20Hoc%20Teamwork%20Research\">A Survey of Ad Hoc Teamwork Research</a>. <em>EUMAS 2022</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"4\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Rahman et al., 2023. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A%20General%20Learning%20Framework%20for%20Open%20Ad%20Hoc%20Teamwork%20Using%20Graph-based%20Policy%20Learning\">A General Learning Framework for Open Ad Hoc Teamwork Using Graph-based Policy Learning</a>. <em>JMLR 2023</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"24\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Li et al., 2023. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Cooperative%20Open-ended%20Learning%20Framework%20for%20Zero-shot%20Coordination\">Cooperative Open-ended Learning Framework for Zero-shot Coordination</a>. <em>ICML 2023</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"46\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Zhao et al., 2023. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Maximum%20Entropy%20Population-Based%20Training%20for%20Zero-Shot%20Human-AI%20Coordination\">Maximum Entropy Population-Based Training for Zero-Shot Human-AI Coordination</a>. <em>AAAI 2023</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"61\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Wang et al., 2024. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=N-Agent%20Ad%20Hoc%20Teamwork\">N-Agent Ad Hoc Teamwork</a>. <em>NeurIPS 2024</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"27\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Wang et al., 2024. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Open%20Ad%20Hoc%20Teamwork%20with%20Cooperative%20Game%20Theory\">Open Ad Hoc Teamwork with Cooperative Game Theory</a>. <em>ICML 2024</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"28\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Suresh et al., 2024. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Open%20Human-Robot%20Collaboration%20using%20Decentralized%20Inverse%20Reinforcement%20Learning\">Open Human-Robot Collaboration using Decentralized Inverse Reinforcement Learning</a>. <em>IROS 2024</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"29\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Li et al., 2025. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=AT-Drone%3A%20Benchmarking%20Adaptive%20Teaming%20in%20Multi-Drone%20Pursuit\">AT-Drone: Benchmarking Adaptive Teaming in Multi-Drone Pursuit</a>. <em>CoRL 2025</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"114\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n<li>Li et al., 2026. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Multi-Robot%20Open%20Adaptive%20Teaming%20Across%20Unseen%20Environments%2C%20Partners%2C%20and%20Scales\">Multi-Robot Open Adaptive Teaming Across Unseen Environments, Partners, and Scales</a>. <em>arXiv 2026</em>. <button class=\"lnk plk\" data-pl=\"31\" title=\"Open the card\">card</button></li>\n</ol></div>");
